  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe main story through Tuesday will be strong southerly winds and \ndry conditions. Slow moving surface high pressure to the east and a \ndeepening low pressure over the Northern Rockies will bring a strong \nsurface pressure gradient to the Northern and Central Plains. Model \nsounding mixed layer suggests winds should gust to 25 to 35 mph on \nMonday and 35 to 45 mph on Tuesday. Both days should see elevated \nfire weather concerns, with the surface relative humidity being the \nonly real limiting factor with afternoon minimum relative humidity\nonly falling to 55 to 65 percent.\n\nSurface low pressure moves onto the Plains Wednesday which will keep \nvery strong winds in place, likely strongest east of Highway 81.\n\nWednesday afternoon and evening will see a fairly dynamic wave swing \nthrough. Rapid cooling aloft in the 700-500mb layer may bring a \nbrief period of instability as a cold front swings through at the \nsurface. The combination of this lift along the front and the \ncooling aloft may allow a band of showers and possibly a rumble of \nthunder or two to develop, but nothing severe. Even small hail looks \nvery unlikely if a small thunderstorm could develop as CAPE values \nare only around 100-250 J/kg and the freezing level should be around \n7 kft. The challenge will be whether or not the system closes off or \njust swings through as an open wave. Without closing off the cooling \naloft may not be enough to allow showers to develop.\n\nBeyond the shower and very isolated thunder threat Wednesday will be \nstrong winds shifting from the south to the west in the afternoon \nand evening. As they turn to the west, gusts in the evening and \nearly overnight hours could be around 40 mph.\n\nLater Wednesday night into Thursday night a quick upper level ridge \nmoves through and mainly dry conditions are expected. The next wave \nwill move into the area Friday into Friday night, with showers and \nisolated thunderstorms possible. Current trends deepen the low \nacross ND Friday night into Saturday, then move east into Sunday. \nOverall, at least precipitation-wise, this should not be a very\nimpactful event. Should remain all rain with only a very small \nthreat for snow later Friday into Saturday as the system moves \neast. With the low wrapping up to the north and shifting east \nand northeast there will likely not be much cold air to wrap in \nbehind. Also, even if this system can produce a couple of inches\nof rain, rivers are expected to remain within their banks.",
